# Local buffer pattern

The single most important data-plane decision: **do not use one store-and-forward
buffer for all telemetry.** The two lanes have opposite requirements.

| Lane | Content | Loss tolerance | Buffer design |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Observability** | Latency, util, throughput, drift/confidence scores | Loss-tolerant — downsample/drop old on overflow | Small, ring / WAL, cheap |
| **Capture (flywheel)** | Inference input + output for fine-tuning | **Loss-averse** — a dropped triggered capture is permanently lost | Durable, disk-backed, **priority-evicted**, QoS 1 |

## Hard rules

1. **Never hold the only buffer copy in RAM** across power cycles — stream from
   **disk** (SQLite/WAL for metrics lane; durable queue for captures).
2. Evict by **value, not age**, and drain by **priority, not FIFO**, on reconnect.
3. Always buffer cheap fields: inference **output**, capture metadata, and the
   **correlation / inference ID** that joins input↔output.
4. Buffer heavy raw **input** only when a product-defined trigger fires
   (drift / OOD / ODD gate), under an explicit disk cap.
5. Reduce high-frequency streams **at the edge before buffering** — per slot /
   per metric, not one global rate. Keep a short full-resolution local ring so
   a trigger can retroactively grab what the model saw.

## Payload asymmetry

- Output is small — prefer keeping it.
- Input may be large — it is what fills disk during outage; apply caps and
  reservoir sampling under pressure so a representative spread is retained,
  not only the newest window.

## Implementation notes

- Metrics → MQTT glue is accepted cost when a single broker uplink is chosen.
- Prefer Python-native scrape + disk queue unless rich tracing later forces a
  companion collector — either way, **disk, not RAM**.

